WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:05.920
Say the government is handing out money, or access to government property or some other benefit.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.160
Can it exclude certain kinds of speech or certain kinds of speakers? It's complicated.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.480
But here are the five rules of the First Amendment and government property.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.460
Rule one. A few forms of government property are treated as so-called traditional public forums.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.340
There the government generally can't exclude speech based on its content.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.440
The classic examples are sidewalks and parks, as well as streets used for parades.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.060
And less speech falls within one of the narrow First Amendment exceptions such as your threats of crime,

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.700
or face-to-face insults that tend to provoke a fight, the government can't restrict it.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.900
Such places are technically government property but that gives the government no extra authority

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.040
to control such speech. The postal system is analogous.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.120
At least since the 1940s the Supreme Court has held that the government can't exclude

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.460
certain kinds of content from the mail. To quote Justice Holmes in an early case,

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.000
"the United States may give up the post office when it sees fit, but while it carries it on,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.380
the use of the mails is almost as much a part of free speech as the right to use our tongues.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.220
Rule two. Sometimes the government deliberately opens up property or funds

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:07.800
in order to promote a wide diversity of private speech using objective criteria.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.440
Many public schools for instance let student groups use classrooms that weren't otherwise being used.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.800
Public libraries often offer rooms for meetings of community groups.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.940
Public universities might offer free email accounts or web hosting to all students

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.920
and sometimes public universities offer money to student groups to publish newspapers

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.540
or invite speakers. These are so-called limited public forums and the government can limit them

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:33.220
to particular speakers, for instance just students, or to particular kinds of speech. For instance,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:38.820
just speech related to the university curriculum. It can also have reasonable viewpoint-neutral exclusions.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.460
For instance saying that certain benefits or property

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.420
can't be used for promoting or opposing candidates into public office.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:50.740
But it can't impose viewpoint-based criteria. It can't for instance let all groups use a meeting room in a library

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.960
but exclude racist groups.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.100
Rule three. A lot of government property is open to the public but not for speech.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.520
Airports for instance are set up to promote transportation, not speaking.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.040
But people there will wear t-shirts with messages on them,

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.440
talk to friends, maybe even approach strangers with belief lights in these so-called non-public forums.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.720
The rule is much like in limited public forums speech restrictions are allowed but must be reasonable,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.700
and viewpoint-neutral.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.940
Rule four. Some government property is set up for the government itself to speak.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.900
And there the government can pick and choose what viewpoints it conveys or endorses.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.160
The walls of most public buildings are an example the government can choose what art to put up there,

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.760
and it might refuse to display art that conveys ideas that it dislikes.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.680
Likewise, when the government spends money to promote its own messages,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.260
it doesn't have to promote rival messages. It can have a National Endowment for democracy

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.040
without having to fund a National Endowment for communism.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.820
It can put out ads supporting racial equality without paying for ads supporting racism.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.060
Sometimes there are close cases. For instance when Texas authorized many kinds of license plate designs,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:57.140
but excluded Confederate flag designs the court split 5 to 4. The majority thought license plate designs

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.580
were government speech and the government could pick and choose which ones to allow.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.900
Even when the government accepted dozens of designs requested by private groups,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.340
the dissent thought they were limited in public forum in which viewpoint discrimination was forbidden

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:14.200
because the government was supporting so many different and often contradictory forms of speech.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.340
But while there were close cases, many are pretty clear. The government often clearly promotes views

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:24.000
it shows itself and sometimes clearly promotes a wide range of private views.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.720
Rule five. Similar principles likely applied to government benefit programs and not just a provision of real estate

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:34.500
or money. Charitable tax exemptions for instance are likely a form of limited public forum.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.060
The government can discriminate based on content. You can't use tax-deductible donations to support

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.800
or oppose candidates for office, but not based on a viewpoint.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.380
Likewise when the Supreme Court held that the government can't deny full trademark protection

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.300
to trademarks that are seen as disparaging, scandalous,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:55.140
immoral, or racist. Such restrictions the court said were impermissibly viewpoint based.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.000
Of course private property owners aren't bound by the First Amendment whether they're distributing money

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.780
or access to real estate. And as we see the government as property owner isn't bound by the First Amendment

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:09.640
quite the same as it is when deciding whether to jail people or find them for their speech.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.860
But except when it comes to the government's own speech viewpoint discrimination is generally forbidden

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.880
even on government property. So to sum up, the government generally can't exclude speech

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.180
based on its content in traditional public forums. The government can deliberately open up

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.700
limited public forums that are restricted to particular speakers or kinds of speech but it can't impose a

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.980
viewpoint-based criteria. In non-public forums speech restrictions are allowed but must be reasonable

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:38.780
and viewpoint-neutral. For government property set up for the government itself to speak,

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.780
the government can pick and choose what viewpoints it conveys or endorses, similar principles likely apply

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.760
to government benefit programs and not just the use of physical property.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.000
This is not legal advice if this were legal advice it would be followed by a bill. Please use responsibly.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.080
Written by Eugene Volokh who is an actual real-life First Amendment law professor at UCLA.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.560
I'm Eugene Volokh and I approve this message.

